ShroudForge loads each Lua mod as an independent package. A package uses <code>mod.json</code> for its identity, requested capabilities, and process targets. Its <code>src/mod.lua</code> contains the mod logic. An optional <code>extended.mod.json</code> declares player-facing settings, action buttons, and links.

== Start with a working package ==

Follow [[Build your first mod]] to create the package folders, write a minimal manifest, and confirm that ShroudForge can find the mod. Read [[mod.json]] before requesting capabilities. Ask for only what the mod needs and explain why it needs it.

== Choose the process that owns the behavior ==

A mod's target determines which Enshrouded process loads its code. A client mod runs on that player's game. A server-targeted mod runs on the Dedicated Server or the host process. Target selection does not synchronize client changes or grant server authority. A feature that changes shared world state must execute in the process that owns that state.

Install a multiplayer mod in every process required by its design and package declaration. Read [[Quickstart: Shroudforge]] and the mod's own installation notes before changing a server installation.

== Add settings and diagnose loading ==

Declare player-facing values in [[extended.mod.json]]. Give each setting a stable key, a useful label, a safe starting value, and limits that match the behavior. Keep Modloader preferences separate from settings owned by an individual mod.

Open the ShroudForge Mods window with <kbd>F9</kbd>. Use <kbd>F10</kbd> to read loader and mod messages. If a mod does not appear, check its folder, manifest, targets, dependencies, and declared capabilities. Some changes need a game restart. The manifest and settings articles explain those conditions.

== Look up exact Lua functions separately ==

This article explains the package and runtime concepts. The complete searchable Lua, native, game-type, and resource reference remains on the [https://bonsaibauer.github.io/shroudforge/en/#api ShroudForge API reference]. Its function signatures and support data are maintained there. The Wiki does not copy its API-symbol pages.

